97: We ﬁrst provide a brief introduction to Li et al. (2018) that reveal the relationship between the optimal control theory and deep learning. We borrow the theorems in Section 4 of Li et al. (2018) and directly follow their notations: Theorem E.1 (discrete-time PMP) Consider the discrete-time control problem where Φ is the termination loss and L is the running loss. Then there exists a co-process such that H Here g t ( x t , θ t ) := x t + δf t ( x t , θ t ) and 19) is the scaled discrete Hamiltonian. Theorem E.2 (discrete-time MSA) The co-process in Theorem E.1 can be determined by applying the discrete-time method of successive approximations (MSA). For each iteration k , set x k 0 = x , and (20) with t enumerating from 0 to T − 1 ; then set p kT = −∇ x Φ( x kT ) , and with t enumerating from T − 1 to 0 ; ﬁnally, with t enumerating from 0 to T − 1 , set Theorem E.3 (equivalence between MSA and back-propagation) The MSA in Theorem E.2 is equiv- alent to gradient descent with back-propagation. The proofs of Theorems E.1, E.2 and E.3 are provided in Li et al. (2018). We now investigate the forward propagation of preﬁx-tuning. In Eq. (9), LM ( j )Φ , the j -th layer of the LM, can be decomposed into a self-attention layer ( SAN ( j )Φ ) and a FFN layer ( FFN ( j )Φ ). Formally, (23) with h ( j ) i = P θ ( j ) [ i, :] for i ∈ P idx . As Eq. (23) is recursive and according to the fact that the pretrained LM is autoregressive, after unrolling the recursion for all h ( j ) <o in Eq. (23), h ( j +1) o can be represented in the form of (24) Now we take Eq. (10), the objective of preﬁx-tuning, into consideration. The optimization problem is now formulated as 27 Using Theorem E.1, we know that the objective of preﬁx-tuning can be formulated as a discrete- time control problem. We then use the MSA described in Theorem E.2 to determine the co-process that solves the control problem and realize that the MSA is equivalent to back-propagation with Theorem E.3, which recovers the training phase of preﬁx-tuning. As a result, we can conclude that preﬁx-tuning seeks the optimal control of the pretrained models for speciﬁc downstream tasks. Our robust preﬁx-tuning framework can also be formalized as the close-loop control of the obtained preﬁx for robust downstream tasks. According to Chen et al. (2021), the close-loop control frame- work for robust neural networks is deﬁned as where L is the running loss. According to Eq. (11), the goal of our robust preﬁx-tuning framework can be formulated into the form Since Theorems E.1, E.2 and E.3 can also be applied to Eq. (27), the optimization of our robust preﬁx is equivalent to seeking the close-loop control for robust downstream tasks. In conclusion, recent years have witnessed the paradigm shift in NLP from adapting LMs to down- stream tasks to adapting downstream tasks to LMs (Liu et al., 2021). In this work, we provide the interpretation of preﬁx-tuning as well as our robust preﬁx-tuning framework from the optimal con- trol perspective. We hope that our work can bring insight to the future design of robust and efﬁcient ﬁnetuning approaches for large-scale pretrained models. 28
